//! TCP connections (RFC 9293): the state machine, data in both directions,
//! retransmission (RFC 6298), and the table that holds a fixed number of
//! connections.
//!
//! The table is a value with no clock, no randomness and no device. Whoever
//! drives it hands it each segment received for this host, with its
//! addresses and the time, a `SegmentSink` that takes the segments it sends,
//! and an `IsnSource` that answers the initial sequence number of each new
//! connection (RFC 9293 §3.4.1, RFC 6528), which needs a clock and a secret
//! the table does not have. Timers run on the time the caller passes to
//! `poll`, and nothing else ages.
//!
//! # The states
//!
//! The eleven states and every transition of RFC 9293's state diagram
//! (§3.3.2): the active open, the passive open through a listener, the
//! simultaneous open, the orderly close from either side or both at once, the
//! reset, and TIME-WAIT's end after `TIME_WAIT_MS`. A segment is taken through
//! the steps of §3.10.7, and one that fails a step is dropped, answered as that
//! step says and reported by its reason (`TcpDrop`).
//!
//! A reset is accepted when its own sequence number is in the receive window
//! (RFC 9293 §3.5.3), wherever the rest of the segment lies, and a SYN whose
//! sequence number is in the window of a synchronized connection ends it with
//! a reset (RFC 793's rule). A SYN before the window, on a segment whose data
//! reaches into it, is old and is trimmed off. RFC 5961's narrower checks, an
//! exact sequence number for a reset and a challenge acknowledgment in reply
//! to an in-window reset or SYN, are not made.
//!
//! A listener's connections are handed out by `accept` once their handshake
//! is done, each once. A listener that closes, aborts or is turned into a
//! connection aborts those it has not handed out.
//!
//! A connection that ends keeps its slot's id answering why until the slot
//! is taken again: `CallError.Reset` after a reset, `CallError.TimedOut`
//! after its retransmissions, and `CallError.NoConnection` after an orderly
//! close or `abort`.
//!
//! # Sending
//!
//! `write` copies into the connection's send buffer (`SEND_BUFFER` bytes),
//! which holds what is unacknowledged and what is not yet sent. Data goes in
//! segments of at most the peer's maximum segment size, but never less than
//! `MIN_SEND_MSS`, a floor below a tiny offer that is kept on purpose; inside
//! the window the peer offers; and the segment that empties
//! the buffer carries PSH: every write is pushed. RFC 9293 §3.8.6.2.1's sender
//! rule avoids silly window syndrome, with the Nagle algorithm: a segment
//! shorter than the maximum goes only when nothing is in flight and either it
//! empties the buffer or it is half the largest window the peer has offered,
//! and data held back that way with nothing in flight goes after
//! `SWS_OVERRIDE_MS`. `close` queues a FIN behind the data. Data written
//! before the handshake ends waits for the SYN's acknowledgment, and a FIN
//! behind it waits too; a close in SYN-RECEIVED with nothing queued sends its
//! FIN at once (RFC 9293 §3.10.4). There is no congestion control: the peer's
//! window is the only limit on what is in flight.
//!
//! # Receiving
//!
//! Data received in order is kept in the receive buffer (`RECEIVE_BUFFER`
//! bytes) until `read` takes it; `read` answers 0 only at the end of the
//! stream, and so refuses a buffer of no bytes. The window offered is the
//! room left behind the right edge last advertised, so it shrinks as data
//! waits and the edge never moves left. Reading moves the edge right only
//! once the room it would add is half the buffer or a maximum segment (RFC
//! 9293 §3.8.6.2.2), and the move is announced with an acknowledgment. A
//! segment's data past the window is trimmed off, and with the window closed
//! only a segment at RCV.NXT is taken, for its acknowledgment and its
//! controls.
//!
//! In-window data or a FIN past the next sequence number expected is not
//! kept for later: it is acknowledged with what is expected, and the peer
//! sends it again.
//!
//! # Retransmission
//!
//! Each connection has one retransmission timer, running while anything sent
//! is unacknowledged (RFC 6298 §5). The timeout starts at `INITIAL_RTO_MS`,
//! follows the smoothed round trip and its variation, measured on one segment
//! at a time and never on a retransmitted one (Karn's algorithm), and lies
//! between the table's minimum (`MIN_RTO_MS`, unless the table was made with
//! another) and `MAX_RTO_MS`. When it expires the earliest unacknowledged
//! segment is sent again, inside the peer's window, the SYN and the SYN,ACK
//! and the FIN among them, the timeout doubles, and sending resumes from
//! there. A connection whose handshake took a retransmission starts its data
//! at `HANDSHAKE_RTO_MS`. Whether the SYN is owed or acknowledged is kept as
//! its own state, never read off sequence numbers, which come round to ISS.
//!
//! The next expiry after the last retry gives a connection up: it is CLOSED,
//! and calls on its id answer `CallError.TimedOut`. An active open's SYN has
//! `SYN_RETRIES`, three minutes; a listener's SYN,ACK has `SYN_ACK_RETRIES`,
//! about half a minute; anything later has `DATA_RETRIES`. A connection a
//! listener spawned whose SYN,ACK is never answered is freed that way. That
//! bounds how long a SYN that is never completed holds a slot, not how many
//! slots such SYNs hold: a stream of them, one per slot in each half minute,
//! still keeps every slot taken.
//!
//! Not here: congestion control, keeping out-of-order data, probing a
//! window that stays closed, keepalives, and FIN-WAIT-2's and TIME-WAIT's
//! fuller handling.
//!
//! # Output
//!
//! A segment goes to `SegmentSink.send`, built whole with its checksum. What a
//! connection owes (its handshake, its data, its FIN, an acknowledgment) is
//! kept as its state, so a segment the sink refuses is owed still, and the
//! next `output` sends it. An abort's reset is owed the same way, and its
//! slot is not taken again until it has gone. A reset answering a segment,
//! and an acknowledgment answering one outside the window, are sent at once,
//! and one the sink refuses is lost as the network may lose one. An
//! acknowledgment with no data, and a reset, carry SND.MAX.

import netstack.addr.{Ipv4Addr, SocketAddr}
import netstack.bytes.{elapsed}
import netstack.ether
import netstack.ipv4
import netstack.tcp
import netstack.tcp.{ACK, FIN, Header, PSH, RST, SYN, TcpDrop}
import netstack.udp.{EPHEMERAL_FIRST, EPHEMERAL_LAST}

/// How many connections, listeners included, the table holds.
public static CONNECTIONS: Int = 8

/// The bytes a connection holds of what it was given to send: sent and
/// unacknowledged, and not yet sent.
public static SEND_BUFFER: Int = 2048

/// The bytes a connection holds of what it received in order and was not
/// read yet: the largest window it offers.
public static RECEIVE_BUFFER: Int = 4096

static_assert(RECEIVE_BUFFER <= 65535, "the window field is 16 bits")

/// The smallest segment sent, whatever size the peer offers. This departs
/// from the peer's offer on purpose, as common stacks do: a peer offering a
/// tiny size would have the stack spend a segment, and its timers, on every
/// few bytes.
public static MIN_SEND_MSS: UInt16 = 64

/// The largest segment sent: this stack's own size, with the fixed header.
static SEGMENT_BYTES: Int = ether.MTU - ipv4.HEADER_BYTES

/// The maximum segment lifetime (RFC 9293 §3.4.2).
public static MSL_MS: UInt64 = 120_000

/// How long a connection stays in TIME-WAIT: twice the maximum segment
/// lifetime, so the last ACK can be repeated for any FIN that can still
/// arrive.
public static TIME_WAIT_MS: UInt64 = 2 * MSL_MS

/// The retransmission timeout before any round trip is measured (RFC 6298
/// §2.1).
public static INITIAL_RTO_MS: UInt64 = 1000

/// The least retransmission timeout (RFC 6298 §2.4), for a table made with
/// `Table.empty`.
public static MIN_RTO_MS: UInt64 = 1000

/// The greatest retransmission timeout, backed off or computed (RFC 6298
/// §2.5).
public static MAX_RTO_MS: UInt64 = 60_000

/// The timeout data starts at when the handshake needed a retransmission
/// (RFC 6298 §5.7).
public static HANDSHAKE_RTO_MS: UInt64 = 3000

/// The retransmissions of an active open's SYN before it is given up: with
/// the backoff from `INITIAL_RTO_MS` to `MAX_RTO_MS`, the last expiry is
/// three minutes after the first SYN (RFC 9293 §3.8.3's R2 for a SYN).
public static SYN_RETRIES: UInt32 = 7

/// The retransmissions of a listener's SYN,ACK before its connection is
/// freed: 31 seconds from the first at `INITIAL_RTO_MS`, which bounds how
/// long a SYN that is never completed holds a slot.
public static SYN_ACK_RETRIES: UInt32 = 4

/// The retransmissions of the same data or FIN before the connection is
/// given up.
public static DATA_RETRIES: UInt32 = 8

/// How long data the silly window rule held back waits, with nothing in
/// flight, before what fits in the window goes anyway (RFC 9293 §3.8.6.2.1's
/// override timeout).
public static SWS_OVERRIDE_MS: UInt64 = 200

/// A connection's state (RFC 9293 §3.3.2). A slot that holds no connection
/// is CLOSED.
public enum State: UInt8 {
    case Closed = 0,
    case Listen = 1,
    case SynSent = 2,
    case SynReceived = 3,
    case Established = 4,
    case FinWait1 = 5,
    case FinWait2 = 6,
    case Closing = 7,
    case TimeWait = 8,
    case CloseWait = 9,
    case LastAck = 10,
}

@synthesize
extension State: Equatable {}

extension State {
    /// The state's name as the RFC spells it.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Closed -> "CLOSED",
            case Listen -> "LISTEN",
            case SynSent -> "SYN-SENT",
            case SynReceived -> "SYN-RECEIVED",
            case Established -> "ESTABLISHED",
            case FinWait1 -> "FIN-WAIT-1",
            case FinWait2 -> "FIN-WAIT-2",
            case Closing -> "CLOSING",
            case TimeWait -> "TIME-WAIT",
            case CloseWait -> "CLOSE-WAIT",
            case LastAck -> "LAST-ACK",
        }
    }
}

extension State: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

/// A refusal from a `SegmentSink`: it has no room for the segment.
public enum SegmentError {
    case Full,
}

/// Where the table's segments go: one call per segment, from `src` to `dst`,
/// the whole TCP segment lent for the call. A sink that answers `Full` has
/// not taken the segment.
public trait SegmentSink {
    func send(&var self, src: Ipv4Addr, dst: Ipv4Addr,
              segment: &[UInt8]) -> Result<Void, SegmentError>
}

/// The initial sequence number of a connection between `local` and `remote`.
/// RFC 6528 asks for a clock-driven counter plus a keyed hash of the four
/// addresses, so an off-path host cannot guess it.
public trait IsnSource {
    func isn(&var self, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr) -> UInt32
}

/// Why a listener or a connection cannot be opened.
public enum OpenError {
    /// A port of zero, or an address of 0.0.0.0 or one that cannot be sent
    /// to.
    case BadAddress,
    /// A listener on the port, or a connection between the two addresses,
    /// exists already.
    case InUse,
    /// Every slot is in use.
    case Full,
    /// The id is not a listener's.
    case NotListening,
    /// The interface has no address yet. Only a stack answers this.
    case NotConfigured,
    /// No secret was given for the initial sequence numbers. Only a stack
    /// answers this.
    case NoSecret,
}

@synthesize
extension OpenError: Equatable {}

extension OpenError {
    /// What a transcript should call this refusal.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case BadAddress -> "an address it cannot open",
            case InUse -> "in use",
            case Full -> "no free connection",
            case NotListening -> "not a listener",
            case NotConfigured -> "no address",
            case NoSecret -> "no secret for the sequence numbers",
        }
    }
}

extension OpenError: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

/// Why a call on a connection is refused.
public enum CallError {
    /// No connection has the id: it was never opened, it ended in an orderly
    /// way or by `abort`, or its slot holds another connection now.
    case NoConnection,
    /// The connection is closing already (RFC 9293 §3.10.4): no more is
    /// written.
    case Closing,
    /// Nothing can be done yet: no data to read, no room to write into, or
    /// no connection to accept. A later call may succeed.
    case WouldBlock,
    /// The id is a listener's, which carries no data.
    case Listening,
    /// The id is not a listener's: only a listener accepts.
    case NotListening,
    /// The connection ended with a reset: one the peer sent, or a SYN in its
    /// window.
    case Reset,
    /// The connection was given up after its retransmissions went
    /// unanswered.
    case TimedOut,
    /// A read into no bytes, which could not tell data waiting from the end
    /// of the stream.
    case NoRoom,
}

@synthesize
extension CallError: Equatable {}

extension CallError {
    /// What a transcript should call this refusal.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case NoConnection -> "no such connection",
            case Closing -> "closing",
            case WouldBlock -> "would block",
            case Listening -> "a listener",
            case NotListening -> "not a listener",
            case Reset -> "reset",
            case TimedOut -> "timed out",
            case NoRoom -> "no room to read into",
        }
    }
}

extension CallError: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

/// What a connection's state, sequence numbers and timers are, for whoever
/// drives the table.
public struct Status {
    public state: State,
    public local: SocketAddr,
    public remote: SocketAddr,
    /// The oldest sequence number sent and not acknowledged.
    public snd_una: UInt32,
    /// The next sequence number to send.
    public snd_nxt: UInt32,
    /// The window the peer last offered.
    public snd_wnd: UInt16,
    /// The next sequence number expected.
    public rcv_nxt: UInt32,
    /// The window offered: the room left up to the right edge advertised.
    public rcv_wnd: UInt16,
    /// The largest segment the peer accepts, no larger than this stack's and
    /// no smaller than `MIN_SEND_MSS`.
    public send_mss: UInt16,
    /// The data bytes received in order.
    public received: UInt64,
    /// The bytes waiting to be read.
    public readable: Int,
    /// The bytes in the send buffer: unacknowledged, and not yet sent.
    public queued: Int,
    /// The retransmission timeout.
    public rto_ms: UInt64,
    /// The smoothed round trip, and its variation; both 0 before the first
    /// measurement.
    public srtt_ms: UInt64,
    public rttvar_ms: UInt64,
    /// The retransmissions since anything new was acknowledged.
    public retries: UInt32,
}

/// Whether `a` comes before `b` in sequence space, which wraps: within half
/// of it, `a` is before `b` when `b - a` is positive.
public func seq_lt(a: UInt32, b: UInt32) -> Bool {
    Int32.from(truncating: a &- b) < 0
}

/// Whether `a` is `b` or comes before it.
public func seq_le(a: UInt32, b: UInt32) -> Bool {
    not seq_lt(b, a)
}

/// The sequence space a segment occupies: its data, and one for each of SYN
/// and FIN.
func occupies(header: &Header, data: Int) -> UInt32 {
    var n = data as UInt32
    if header.has(SYN) {
        n = n + 1
    }
    if header.has(FIN) {
        n = n + 1
    }
    n
}

/// The largest segment to send a peer that offered `offered`, or nothing.
func send_mss(offered: UInt16?) -> UInt16 {
    let peer = offered ?? tcp.DEFAULT_MSS
    if peer < MIN_SEND_MSS {
        MIN_SEND_MSS
    } else if peer < tcp.OFFERED_MSS {
        peer
    } else {
        tcp.OFFERED_MSS
    }
}

/// The ephemeral port after `port`, wrapping from the last to the first.
func after(port: UInt16) -> UInt16 {
    if port == EPHEMERAL_LAST { EPHEMERAL_FIRST } else { port + 1 }
}

/// Builds the segment `header` with no data from `src` to `dst` and sends it.
func emit<S: SegmentSink>(src: Ipv4Addr, dst: Ipv4Addr, header: &Header,
                          sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
    var bytes: [UInt8; tcp.MAX_HEADER_BYTES] = [0; tcp.MAX_HEADER_BYTES]
    let n = tcp.write_segment(&var bytes, src, dst, &header, 0)
    sink.send(src, dst, &bytes[..n])
}

/// Answers `header`, a segment with `data` bytes from `remote` to `local`,
/// with a reset (RFC 9293 §3.5.2): one whose sequence number is the
/// segment's acknowledgment when it has one, else a reset with ACK that
/// acknowledges the whole segment, so the sender accepts it in either case.
func reset_reply<S: SegmentSink>(local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr,
                                 header: &Header, data: Int, sink: &var S) {
    let reply = if header.has(ACK) {
        Header(src_port: local.port, dst_port: remote.port, seq: header.ack,
               ack: 0, flags: RST, window: 0, mss: None)
    } else {
        Header(src_port: local.port, dst_port: remote.port, seq: 0,
               ack: header.seq &+ occupies(&header, data), flags: RST | ACK,
               window: 0, mss: None)
    }
    let _ = emit(local.addr, remote.addr, &reply, &var sink)
}

// =============================================================================
// A connection
// =============================================================================

/// How much `output` may send.
enum Push {
    /// What the window and the silly window rule allow.
    case Normal,
    /// One segment from SND.NXT, whatever the window and the rule say: the
    /// retransmission a timeout calls for.
    case Resend,
    /// What the window allows, the silly window rule overridden.
    case Override,
}

@synthesize
extension Push: Equatable {}

/// One slot's connection: RFC 9293's transmission control block (§3.3.1),
/// with its buffers and its timer.
struct Connection {
    state: State,
    /// Spawned by a listener: a reset or a SYN in SYN-RECEIVED frees it, and
    /// the listener goes on listening.
    passive: Bool,
    /// For a listener, the port alone.
    local: SocketAddr,
    remote: SocketAddr,
    iss: UInt32,
    snd_una: UInt32,
    snd_nxt: UInt32,
    /// The sequence number past the last one ever sent: SND.NXT goes back to
    /// SND.UNA on a timeout, and this does not.
    snd_max: UInt32,
    snd_wnd: UInt16,
    /// The largest window the peer has offered.
    max_wnd: UInt16,
    /// The sequence number of the segment that last set the send window.
    snd_wl1: UInt32,
    irs: UInt32,
    rcv_nxt: UInt32,
    /// The receive window's right edge, as last advertised.
    rcv_adv: UInt32,
    send_mss: UInt16,
    received: UInt64,
    /// When TIME-WAIT began.
    time_wait_at: UInt64,
    /// Why the connection ended, once it is CLOSED: what calls on its id
    /// answer.
    ended: CallError,

    /// The send buffer, a ring: `send_len` bytes from `send_head`, the first
    /// at sequence number `data_seq`.
    send_buf: [UInt8; SEND_BUFFER],
    send_head: Int,
    send_len: Int,
    data_seq: UInt32,
    /// CLOSE was called: a FIN follows the data, at `data_seq + send_len`.
    fin_queued: Bool,
    /// The receive buffer, a ring: `recv_len` bytes from `recv_head`.
    recv_buf: [UInt8; RECEIVE_BUFFER],
    recv_head: Int,
    recv_len: Int,
    /// An acknowledgment is owed: the next segment carries it, or one goes
    /// alone.
    ack_due: Bool,

    /// The table's least timeout, and the timeout.
    min_rto: UInt64,
    rto: UInt64,
    srtt: UInt64,
    rttvar: UInt64,
    /// A round trip has been measured.
    sampled: Bool,
    /// The retransmission timer: running, and since when.
    timer_on: Bool,
    timer_from: UInt64,
    /// Timeouts since anything new was acknowledged.
    retries: UInt32,
    /// The segment being timed: its first sequence number, and when it went.
    timing: Bool,
    timed_seq: UInt32,
    timed_at: UInt64,
    /// Data the silly window rule held back with nothing in flight, since
    /// when.
    held: Bool,
    held_at: UInt64,
    /// The SYN or the SYN,ACK was sent again.
    handshake_resent: Bool,
    /// The SYN, or SYN,ACK, is to be sent: it has not been, or a timeout
    /// calls for it again.
    syn_owed: Bool,
    /// The peer has acknowledged the SYN.
    syn_acked: Bool,
    /// The connection was aborted and its reset has not gone yet.
    reset_owed: Bool,

    /// For a connection a listener spawned, the listener's slot, and whether
    /// `accept` has handed it out. A listener that ends aborts what it
    /// spawned and did not hand out, so no slot names a listener that is
    /// gone.
    listener: Int,
    accepted: Bool,
}

extension Connection {
    /// No connection.
    static func free() -> Connection {
        let nowhere = SocketAddr(addr: Ipv4Addr.unspecified(), port: 0)
        Connection(state: State.Closed, passive: false, local: nowhere,
                   remote: nowhere, iss: 0, snd_una: 0, snd_nxt: 0, snd_max: 0,
                   snd_wnd: 0, max_wnd: 0, snd_wl1: 0, irs: 0, rcv_nxt: 0,
                   rcv_adv: 0, send_mss: tcp.DEFAULT_MSS, received: 0,
                   time_wait_at: 0, ended: CallError.NoConnection,
                   send_buf: [0; SEND_BUFFER], send_head: 0, send_len: 0,
                   data_seq: 0, fin_queued: false,
                   recv_buf: [0; RECEIVE_BUFFER], recv_head: 0, recv_len: 0,
                   ack_due: false, min_rto: MIN_RTO_MS, rto: INITIAL_RTO_MS,
                   srtt: 0, rttvar: 0, sampled: false, timer_on: false,
                   timer_from: 0, retries: 0, timing: false, timed_seq: 0,
                   timed_at: 0, held: false, held_at: 0,
                   handshake_resent: false, syn_owed: false,
                   syn_acked: false, reset_owed: false, listener: -1,
                   accepted: false)
    }

    /// A connection in `state` from `local` to `remote` whose SYN is at
    /// `iss` and not sent yet, with the table's least timeout `min_rto`.
    static func begin(state: State, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr,
                      iss: UInt32, min_rto: UInt64) -> Connection {
        var c = Connection.free()
        c.state = state
        c.local = local
        c.remote = remote
        c.iss = iss
        c.snd_una = iss
        c.snd_nxt = iss
        c.snd_max = iss
        c.data_seq = iss &+ 1
        c.syn_owed = true
        c.min_rto = min_rto
        c.rto = if INITIAL_RTO_MS < min_rto { min_rto } else { INITIAL_RTO_MS }
        c
    }

    /// Sends a segment of `flags` with no data. A SYN carries this stack's
    /// segment size, and a reset offers no window.
    func send<S: SegmentSink>(&self, seq: UInt32, ack: UInt32, flags: UInt8,
                              sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
        let header = Header(
            src_port: self.local.port, dst_port: self.remote.port, seq: seq,
            ack: ack, flags: flags,
            window: if (flags & RST) != 0 { 0 } else { self.window() },
            mss: if (flags & SYN) != 0 { tcp.OFFERED_MSS } else { None })
        emit(self.local.addr, self.remote.addr, &header, &var sink)
    }

    /// `<SEQ=SND.MAX><ACK=RCV.NXT><CTL=ACK>`, at once: the answer to a
    /// segment that is not taken. An acknowledgment with no data carries
    /// SND.MAX, where the peer's RCV.NXT is, even while a timeout has taken
    /// SND.NXT back.
    func send_ack<S: SegmentSink>(&self, sink: &var S) {
        let _ = self.send(self.snd_max, self.rcv_nxt, ACK, &var sink)
    }

    /// `<SEQ=ISS><ACK=RCV.NXT><CTL=SYN,ACK>`, at once: the answer to the
    /// peer's SYN repeated.
    func send_syn_ack<S: SegmentSink>(&self, sink: &var S) {
        let _ = self.send(self.iss, self.rcv_nxt, SYN | ACK, &var sink)
    }

    /// The window to offer: the room up to the right edge advertised, or,
    /// before the peer's SYN is known, the room in the buffer.
    func window(&self) -> UInt16 {
        if self.state == State.SynSent {
            (RECEIVE_BUFFER - self.recv_len) as UInt16
        } else {
            (self.rcv_adv &- self.rcv_nxt) as UInt16
        }
    }

    /// Whether `seq` is in the receive window. A closed window holds only
    /// RCV.NXT, so a segment there can still carry an acknowledgment or a
    /// reset (RFC 9293 §3.10.7.4).
    func in_window(&self, seq: UInt32) -> Bool {
        let wnd = self.rcv_adv &- self.rcv_nxt
        if wnd == 0 {
            seq == self.rcv_nxt
        } else {
            (seq &- self.rcv_nxt) < wnd
        }
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.7.4's acceptability test, for a segment at `seq` that
    /// occupies `length` of sequence space: it begins or ends in the window.
    func acceptable(&self, seq: UInt32, length: UInt32) -> Bool {
        if length == 0 {
            self.in_window(seq)
        } else {
            self.in_window(seq) || self.in_window(seq &+ (length - 1))
        }
    }

    /// Takes in the peer's SYN at `header`, as LISTEN and SYN-SENT do.
    func take_syn(&var self, header: &Header) {
        self.irs = header.seq
        self.rcv_nxt = header.seq &+ 1
        let room = (RECEIVE_BUFFER - self.recv_len) as UInt32
        self.rcv_adv = self.rcv_nxt &+ room
        self.send_mss = send_mss(header.mss)
        self.snd_wnd = header.window
        self.max_wnd = header.window
        self.snd_wl1 = header.seq
    }

    /// The handshake is done: data starts at `HANDSHAKE_RTO_MS` when it took
    /// a retransmission (RFC 6298 §5.7).
    func established(&var self) {
        self.state = State.Established
        if self.handshake_resent && not self.sampled {
            self.rto = self.clamp_rto(HANDSHAKE_RTO_MS)
        }
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.7.3: a segment for a connection in SYN-SENT.
    func arrive_syn_sent<S: SegmentSink>(
            &var self, header: &Header, data: &[UInt8], now: UInt64,
            sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, TcpDrop> {
        let ack = header.has(ACK)
        if ack && (seq_le(header.ack, self.iss) ||
                   seq_lt(self.snd_max, header.ack)) {
            if not header.has(RST) {
                reset_reply(self.local, self.remote, &header, data.len(),
                            &var sink)
            }
            return TcpDrop.AckUnsent
        }
        if header.has(RST) {
            if not ack {
                return TcpDrop.ResetIgnored
            }
            self.end(CallError.Reset)
            return TcpDrop.Reset
        }
        if not header.has(SYN) {
            return TcpDrop.NoSyn
        }
        self.take_syn(&header)
        if ack {
            self.take_ack(header.ack, now)
        }
        if self.syn_acked {
            self.established()
            let _ = self.take_text(header.seq &+ 1, header.has(FIN), &data, now)
            self.ack_due = true
            let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        } else {
            // Both ends sent a SYN: each answers the other's with SYN,ACK.
            // Text and FIN on the SYN are not kept; the peer sends them again.
            self.state = State.SynReceived
            self.send_syn_ack(&var sink)
        }
        return
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.7.4: a segment for a connection in SYN-RECEIVED or a
    /// later state, taken through the steps in order.
    func arrive<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, header: &Header, data: &[UInt8],
                                now: UInt64,
                                sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, TcpDrop> {
        if self.state == State.SynSent {
            return self.arrive_syn_sent(&header, &data, now, &var sink)
        }
        let bytes = data.len()
        var seq = header.seq
        var syn = header.has(SYN)
        let rst = header.has(RST)
        let fin = header.has(FIN)
        // SYN-RECEIVED and the peer's SYN again, at the sequence number
        // already taken in. Without ACK it is a retransmission, answered with
        // the SYN,ACK again. With ACK it is the SYN,ACK of a simultaneous
        // open, which establishes the connection (RFC 9293 §3.5, Figure 7):
        // the SYN is old, and the rest of the segment is processed.
        if self.state == State.SynReceived && syn && not rst &&
           seq == self.irs {
            if not header.has(ACK) {
                self.send_syn_ack(&var sink)
                return
            }
            seq = seq &+ 1
            syn = false
        }
        var length = bytes as UInt32
        if syn {
            length = length + 1
        }
        if fin {
            length = length + 1
        }

        // First, the sequence number. A reset counts by its own, not by
        // where its data ends.
        if rst {
            if not self.in_window(seq) {
                return TcpDrop.ResetOutOfWindow
            }
        } else if not self.acceptable(seq, length) {
            self.send_ack(&var sink)
            return TcpDrop.OutOfWindow
        }
        // A SYN before the window, its data reaching into it, is old.
        if syn && not self.in_window(seq) {
            seq = seq &+ 1
            syn = false
        }

        // Second, the RST bit. A connection a listener spawned is freed, and
        // the listener goes on.
        if rst {
            self.end(CallError.Reset)
            return TcpDrop.Reset
        }

        // Fourth, the SYN bit (the third, security, has nothing to check). A
        // spawned connection in SYN-RECEIVED is freed without a word, as it
        // returns to LISTEN; any other is reset.
        if syn {
            if not (self.state == State.SynReceived && self.passive) {
                reset_reply(self.local, self.remote, &header, bytes, &var sink)
            }
            self.end(CallError.Reset)
            return TcpDrop.UnexpectedSyn
        }

        // Fifth, the ACK field.
        if not header.has(ACK) {
            return TcpDrop.NoAck
        }
        if self.state == State.SynReceived {
            if not (seq_lt(self.snd_una, header.ack) &&
                    seq_le(header.ack, self.snd_max)) {
                reset_reply(self.local, self.remote, &header, bytes, &var sink)
                return TcpDrop.AckUnsent
            }
            self.established()
            self.snd_wnd = header.window
            self.snd_wl1 = seq
        }
        if seq_lt(self.snd_max, header.ack) {
            self.send_ack(&var sink)
            return TcpDrop.AckUnsent
        }
        if seq_lt(self.snd_una, header.ack) {
            self.take_ack(header.ack, now)
        }
        // The window moves with a segment no older than the one that last
        // moved it, unless its acknowledgment is a duplicate, below SND.UNA.
        // RFC 9293's test on SND.WL2 is implied: the acknowledgment that last
        // moved the window is at or below SND.UNA.
        if seq_le(self.snd_una, header.ack) && seq_le(self.snd_wl1, seq) {
            self.snd_wnd = header.window
            self.snd_wl1 = seq
            if header.window > self.max_wnd {
                self.max_wnd = header.window
            }
        }
        match self.state {
            case FinWait1 -> {
                if self.fin_acked() {
                    self.state = State.FinWait2
                }
            },
            case Closing -> {
                if not self.fin_acked() {
                    // The FIN is not acknowledged: what is unsent before it,
                    // and the FIN itself, may still be owed.
                    let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
                    return
                }
                self.state = State.TimeWait
                self.time_wait_at = now
                self.timer_on = false
            },
            case LastAck -> {
                if self.fin_acked() {
                    self.end(CallError.NoConnection)
                    return
                }
            },
            case _ -> {},
        }

        // Seventh and eighth, the text and the FIN, kept only in order.
        if (bytes != 0 || fin) && self.takes_text() &&
           seq_lt(self.rcv_nxt, seq) {
            // Answered with what is expected, on whatever data its
            // acknowledgment let go.
            self.ack_due = true
            let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
            return TcpDrop.OutOfOrder
        }
        if self.take_text(seq, fin, &data, now) {
            self.ack_due = true
        }
        let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return
    }

    /// Ends the connection for `why`, which calls on its id answer.
    func end(&var self, why: CallError) {
        self.state = State.Closed
        self.ended = why
        self.timer_on = false
    }

    /// Takes in an acknowledgment of `ack`, past SND.UNA and at most
    /// SND.MAX: frees the data it covers, measures the round trip when it
    /// covers the segment being timed, and restarts the timer for what is
    /// still unacknowledged, or stops it (RFC 6298 §5.2, §5.3).
    func take_ack(&var self, ack: UInt32, now: UInt64) {
        if seq_lt(self.data_seq, ack) {
            let covered = (ack &- self.data_seq) as Int
            let n = covered.min(self.send_len)
            self.send_head = (self.send_head + n) % SEND_BUFFER
            self.send_len = self.send_len - n
            self.data_seq = self.data_seq &+ (n as UInt32)
        }
        self.snd_una = ack
        self.syn_acked = true
        if seq_lt(self.snd_nxt, ack) {
            self.snd_nxt = ack
        }
        if self.timing && seq_lt(self.timed_seq, ack) {
            self.timing = false
            self.sample(elapsed(now, self.timed_at))
        }
        self.retries = 0
        if self.snd_una == self.snd_max {
            self.timer_on = false
        } else {
            self.timer_from = now
        }
    }

    /// Takes a round-trip measurement of `measured` milliseconds into the
    /// smoothed round trip and its variation, and recomputes the timeout
    /// (RFC 6298 §2, with a clock granularity of a millisecond). A
    /// measurement past `MAX_RTO_MS` counts as that, so the caller's clock
    /// cannot overflow the sums.
    func sample(&var self, measured: UInt64) {
        let r = if measured > MAX_RTO_MS { MAX_RTO_MS } else { measured }
        if self.sampled {
            let diff = if self.srtt > r { self.srtt - r } else { r - self.srtt }
            self.rttvar = (3 * self.rttvar + diff) / 4
            self.srtt = (7 * self.srtt + r) / 8
        } else {
            self.srtt = r
            self.rttvar = r / 2
            self.sampled = true
        }
        let spread = if self.rttvar == 0 { 1 } else { 4 * self.rttvar }
        self.rto = self.clamp_rto(self.srtt + spread)
    }

    /// `rto` held between the table's least timeout and `MAX_RTO_MS`.
    func clamp_rto(&self, rto: UInt64) -> UInt64 {
        if rto < self.min_rto {
            self.min_rto
        } else if rto > MAX_RTO_MS {
            MAX_RTO_MS
        } else {
            rto
        }
    }

    /// Whether the FIN is sent and acknowledged.
    func fin_acked(&self) -> Bool {
        self.fin_queued && self.send_len == 0 &&
            self.snd_una == (self.data_seq &+ 1)
    }

    /// Whether the state takes data and a FIN: the peer has not sent its FIN.
    func takes_text(&self) -> Bool {
        self.state == State.Established || self.state == State.FinWait1 ||
            self.state == State.FinWait2
    }

    /// Whether the peer's FIN has been taken in: what is buffered is all
    /// there will be.
    func peer_finished(&self) -> Bool {
        self.state == State.CloseWait || self.state == State.LastAck ||
            self.state == State.Closing || self.state == State.TimeWait
    }

    /// Takes in `data` and a FIN from `seq`, which is at or before RCV.NXT,
    /// the part before it already taken: as much as the window holds, the FIN
    /// only if all of it fits. Answers whether the segment is to be
    /// acknowledged.
    func take_text(&var self, seq: UInt32, fin: Bool, data: &[UInt8],
                   now: UInt64) -> Bool {
        if not self.takes_text() || (data.len() == 0 && not fin) {
            return false
        }
        // The segment was acceptable, so it reaches RCV.NXT: `skip` is at
        // most its data.
        let skip = (self.rcv_nxt &- seq) as Int
        let fresh = data.len() - skip
        let room = (self.rcv_adv &- self.rcv_nxt) as Int
        let take = fresh.min(room)
        var i = 0
        while i < take {
            let at = (self.recv_head + self.recv_len + i) % RECEIVE_BUFFER
            self.recv_buf[at] = data[skip + i]
            i = i + 1
        }
        self.recv_len = self.recv_len + take
        self.received = self.received + (take as UInt64)
        self.rcv_nxt = self.rcv_nxt &+ (take as UInt32)
        if fin && take == fresh {
            self.rcv_nxt = self.rcv_nxt &+ 1
            self.rcv_adv = self.rcv_adv &+ 1
            match self.state {
                case Established -> self.state = State.CloseWait,
                case FinWait1 -> self.state = State.Closing,
                case _ -> {
                    self.state = State.TimeWait
                    self.time_wait_at = now
                    self.timer_on = false
                },
            }
        }
        true
    }

    /// How much of the peer's window is not in flight.
    func usable(&self) -> Int {
        let edge = self.snd_una &+ (self.snd_wnd as UInt32)
        if seq_lt(self.snd_nxt, edge) {
            (edge &- self.snd_nxt) as Int
        } else {
            0
        }
    }

    /// Notes a segment from `seq` occupying `occupied` of sequence space
    /// sent at `now`: SND.NXT moves past it, the timer starts unless it runs
    /// (RFC 6298 §5.1), and a segment with new data is timed unless one is
    /// timed already. A segment sent before is never timed (Karn).
    func sent(&var self, seq: UInt32, occupied: UInt32, now: UInt64) {
        if not self.timing && seq == self.snd_max {
            self.timing = true
            self.timed_seq = seq
            self.timed_at = now
        }
        self.snd_nxt = seq &+ occupied
        if seq_lt(self.snd_max, self.snd_nxt) {
            self.snd_max = self.snd_nxt
        }
        if not self.timer_on {
            self.timer_on = true
            self.timer_from = now
        }
    }

    /// Sends the SYN, or the SYN,ACK, from ISS.
    func send_syn<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64,
                                  sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
        if self.state == State.SynSent {
            try self.send(self.iss, 0, SYN, &var sink)
        } else {
            try self.send(self.iss, self.rcv_nxt, SYN | ACK, &var sink)
        }
        self.syn_owed = false
        self.sent(self.iss, 1, now)
        return
    }

    /// Sends `n` bytes of the send buffer from `off` at SND.NXT, with
    /// `flags`.
    func send_data<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, off: Int, n: Int, flags: UInt8,
                                   now: UInt64,
                                   sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
        var bytes: [UInt8; SEGMENT_BYTES] = [0; SEGMENT_BYTES]
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let at = (self.send_head + off + i) % SEND_BUFFER
            bytes[tcp.HEADER_BYTES + i] = self.send_buf[at]
            i = i + 1
        }
        let header = Header(src_port: self.local.port,
                            dst_port: self.remote.port, seq: self.snd_nxt,
                            ack: self.rcv_nxt, flags: flags,
                            window: self.window(), mss: None)
        let len = tcp.write_segment(&var bytes, self.local.addr,
                                    self.remote.addr, &header, n)
        try sink.send(self.local.addr, self.remote.addr, &bytes[..len])
        var occupied = n as UInt32
        if (flags & FIN) != 0 {
            occupied = occupied + 1
        }
        self.ack_due = false
        self.held = false
        self.sent(self.snd_nxt, occupied, now)
        return
    }

    /// Sends what the connection owes, as `push` allows: the SYN or SYN,ACK
    /// while it is owed; then data, once the SYN is acknowledged;
    /// then the FIN, once all the data is sent; and an acknowledgment alone
    /// when one is owed and nothing else carried it. Stops at the first
    /// segment the sink refuses, which stays owed.
    func output<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64, sink: &var S,
                                push: Push) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
        if self.state == State.Closed {
            if self.reset_owed {
                try self.send(self.snd_max, 0, RST, &var sink)
                self.reset_owed = false
            }
            return
        }
        if self.state == State.Listen {
            return
        }
        if self.state == State.TimeWait {
            if self.ack_due {
                try self.send(self.snd_max, self.rcv_nxt, ACK, &var sink)
                self.ack_due = false
            }
            return
        }
        if self.syn_owed {
            try self.send_syn(now, &var sink)
            if push == Push.Resend {
                return
            }
        }
        if self.state == State.SynSent || self.state == State.SynReceived {
            return
        }
        while {
            let off = (self.snd_nxt &- self.data_seq) as Int
            let fin_sent = off > self.send_len
            let unsent = if fin_sent { 0 } else { self.send_len - off }
            if unsent > 0 && self.syn_acked {
                let usable = self.usable()
                let mss = self.send_mss as Int
                let n = unsent.min(mss).min(usable)
                let idle = self.snd_nxt == self.snd_una
                let fits = n == mss || (idle && unsent <= usable) ||
                    (idle && 2 * n >= (self.max_wnd as Int))
                if n > 0 && (fits || push != Push.Normal) {
                    let last = off + n == self.send_len
                    var flags = ACK
                    if last {
                        flags = flags | PSH
                        if self.fin_queued {
                            flags = flags | FIN
                        }
                    }
                    try self.send_data(off, n, flags, now, &var sink)
                    if push == Push.Resend {
                        return
                    }
                    continue
                }
                if idle && n > 0 && not self.held {
                    self.held = true
                    self.held_at = now
                }
                break
            }
            if self.fin_queued && not fin_sent && unsent == 0 {
                try self.send_data(off, 0, FIN | ACK, now, &var sink)
                if push == Push.Resend {
                    return
                }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        if self.ack_due {
            try self.send(self.snd_max, self.rcv_nxt, ACK, &var sink)
            self.ack_due = false
        }
        return
    }

    /// Ages the connection at `now`: TIME-WAIT's end, the retransmission
    /// timer, and data the silly window rule has held back too long.
    func tick<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64, sink: &var S) {
        if self.state == State.TimeWait {
            if elapsed(now, self.time_wait_at) >= TIME_WAIT_MS {
                self.end(CallError.NoConnection)
            }
            return
        }
        if self.timer_on && elapsed(now, self.timer_from) >= self.rto {
            self.expire(now, &var sink)
            return
        }
        if self.held && elapsed(now, self.held_at) >= SWS_OVERRIDE_MS {
            let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Override)
        }
    }

    /// The retransmission timer expired at `now` (RFC 6298 §5.4–5.6): the
    /// connection is given up once it has had its retries; otherwise the
    /// timeout doubles, the measurement in progress is dropped, and the
    /// earliest unacknowledged segment goes again.
    func expire<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64, sink: &var S) {
        let handshake = not self.syn_acked
        let limit = if not handshake {
            DATA_RETRIES
        } else if self.passive {
            SYN_ACK_RETRIES
        } else {
            SYN_RETRIES
        }
        self.retries = self.retries + 1
        if self.retries > limit {
            self.end(CallError.TimedOut)
            return
        }
        self.rto = if self.rto >= MAX_RTO_MS / 2 {
            MAX_RTO_MS
        } else {
            2 * self.rto
        }
        self.timing = false
        if handshake {
            self.handshake_resent = true
            self.syn_owed = true
        }
        self.snd_nxt = self.snd_una
        self.timer_from = now
        let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Resend)
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.2: SEND. Copies as much of `data` as the send buffer
    /// has room for and answers how much; before the handshake ends it waits
    /// there.
    func write<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, data: &[UInt8], now: UInt64,
                               sink: &var S) -> Result<Int, CallError> {
        match self.state {
            case Listen -> return CallError.Listening,
            case SynSent -> {},
            case SynReceived -> {},
            case Established -> {},
            case CloseWait -> {},
            case _ -> return CallError.Closing,
        }
        let n = data.len().min(SEND_BUFFER - self.send_len)
        if n == 0 && data.len() != 0 {
            return CallError.WouldBlock
        }
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let at = (self.send_head + self.send_len + i) % SEND_BUFFER
            self.send_buf[at] = data[i]
            i = i + 1
        }
        self.send_len = self.send_len + n
        let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return n
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.3: RECEIVE. Copies the data waiting into `into`, as
    /// much as fits, and answers how much: 0 once the peer's FIN is taken and
    /// nothing is left, which is why an `into` of no bytes is refused
    /// (`NoRoom`). Opening the window this way is announced when it moves the
    /// right edge (RFC 9293 §3.8.6.2.2).
    func read<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, into: &var [UInt8], now: UInt64,
                              sink: &var S) -> Result<Int, CallError> {
        if self.state == State.Listen {
            return CallError.Listening
        }
        if into.len() == 0 {
            return CallError.NoRoom
        }
        if self.recv_len == 0 {
            if self.peer_finished() {
                return 0
            }
            return CallError.WouldBlock
        }
        let n = self.recv_len.min(into.len())
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            into[i] = self.recv_buf[(self.recv_head + i) % RECEIVE_BUFFER]
            i = i + 1
        }
        self.recv_head = (self.recv_head + n) % RECEIVE_BUFFER
        self.recv_len = self.recv_len - n
        if self.takes_text() {
            let room = RECEIVE_BUFFER - self.recv_len
            let offered = (self.rcv_adv &- self.rcv_nxt) as Int
            let worth = (RECEIVE_BUFFER / 2).min(self.send_mss as Int)
            if room - offered >= worth {
                self.rcv_adv = self.rcv_nxt &+ (room as UInt32)
                self.ack_due = true
                let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
            }
        }
        return n
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.4: CLOSE. A synchronized connection queues its FIN
    /// behind its data.
    func close<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64,
                               sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        match self.state {
            case Closed -> return CallError.NoConnection,
            case Listen -> self.end(CallError.NoConnection),
            case SynSent -> self.end(CallError.NoConnection),
            case SynReceived -> {
                self.fin_queued = true
                self.state = State.FinWait1
            },
            case Established -> {
                self.fin_queued = true
                self.state = State.FinWait1
            },
            case CloseWait -> {
                self.fin_queued = true
                self.state = State.LastAck
            },
            case _ -> return CallError.Closing,
        }
        let _ = self.output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.5: ABORT. A connection the peer knows of owes it a
    /// reset, `<SEQ=SND.MAX><CTL=RST>`, which goes now if the sink takes it
    /// and with the next `output` if not; the slot is not reused until it
    /// has gone.
    func abort<S: SegmentSink>(&var self,
                               sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        let known = match self.state {
            case Closed -> { return CallError.NoConnection },
            case SynReceived -> true,
            case Established -> true,
            case FinWait1 -> true,
            case FinWait2 -> true,
            case CloseWait -> true,
            case _ -> false,
        }
        self.end(CallError.NoConnection)
        self.reset_owed = known
        let _ = self.output(0, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return
    }

    /// `Table.skip_sequence`.
    func skip_sequence(&var self, by: UInt32) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        if self.state != State.Established || self.send_len != 0 ||
           self.snd_una != self.snd_max {
            return CallError.WouldBlock
        }
        self.snd_una = self.snd_una &+ by
        self.snd_nxt = self.snd_una
        self.snd_max = self.snd_una
        self.data_seq = self.snd_una
        return
    }

    func status(&self) -> Status {
        Status(state: self.state, local: self.local, remote: self.remote,
               snd_una: self.snd_una, snd_nxt: self.snd_nxt,
               snd_wnd: self.snd_wnd, rcv_nxt: self.rcv_nxt,
               rcv_wnd: self.window(), send_mss: self.send_mss,
               received: self.received, readable: self.recv_len,
               queued: self.send_len, rto_ms: self.rto, srtt_ms: self.srtt,
               rttvar_ms: self.rttvar, retries: self.retries)
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// The table
// =============================================================================

/// A connection's or a listener's id: its slot, and which of the slot's
/// occupants it is. Only the table makes one. Once the slot holds another
/// connection the id names nothing, until the slot's generation wraps.
public struct ConnId {
    private slot: UInt8,
    private generation: UInt32,
}

@synthesize
extension ConnId: Equatable {}

/// The connections and listeners, each in a slot.
public struct Table {
    private slots: [Connection; CONNECTIONS],
    /// Each slot's occupant: counted up each time the slot is taken.
    private generations: [UInt32; CONNECTIONS],
    /// The least retransmission timeout of every connection.
    private min_rto: UInt64,
    /// Where the search for an ephemeral port starts.
    private next_port: UInt16,
}

extension Table {
    /// No connections, with retransmission timeouts of at least
    /// `min_rto_ms`.
    public init(min_rto_ms: UInt64) -> Table {
        Table(slots: [Connection.free(); CONNECTIONS],
              generations: [0; CONNECTIONS], min_rto: min_rto_ms,
              next_port: EPHEMERAL_FIRST)
    }

    /// No connections, with RFC 6298's least timeout, `MIN_RTO_MS`.
    public static func empty() -> Table {
        Table(min_rto_ms: MIN_RTO_MS)
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.1's passive OPEN: a listener on `port`, which spawns a
    /// connection in SYN-RECEIVED for each SYN to the port and goes on
    /// listening. Answers its id.
    public func listen(&var self,
                       port: UInt16) -> Result<ConnId, OpenError> {
        if port == 0 {
            return OpenError.BadAddress
        }
        if self.listener(port) != None {
            return OpenError.InUse
        }
        guard let i = self.free_slot() else { return OpenError.Full }
        var c = Connection.free()
        c.state = State.Listen
        c.local.port = port
        return self.occupy(i, c)
    }

    /// A connection listener `id` spawned whose handshake is done and that
    /// was not handed out before; `WouldBlock` while there is none.
    public func accept(&var self, id: ConnId) -> Result<ConnId, CallError> {
        let l = try self.used(id)
        if self.slots[l].state != State.Listen {
            return CallError.NotListening
        }
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.spawned_by(i, l) && not self.slots[i].accepted &&
               self.slots[i].state != State.SynReceived {
                self.slots[i].accepted = true
                return ConnId(slot: i as UInt8, generation: self.generations[i])
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        return CallError.WouldBlock
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.1's active OPEN from `local` to `remote` at `now`:
    /// sends the SYN and answers the connection's id.
    public func connect<S: SegmentSink, I: IsnSource>(
            &var self, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr, now: UInt64,
            isn: &var I, sink: &var S) -> Result<ConnId, OpenError> {
        try self.check_open(local, remote)
        guard let i = self.free_slot() else { return OpenError.Full }
        let iss = isn.isn(local, remote)
        let id = self.occupy(i, Connection.begin(State.SynSent, local, remote,
                                                 iss, self.min_rto))
        let _ = self.slots[i].output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return id
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.2's SEND to listener `id`, which turns it into an
    /// active open from `addr` at its port to `remote`: the SYN goes out and
    /// the listener is the connection, in SYN-SENT. What it spawned and did
    /// not hand out is aborted, as when it closes.
    public func connect_listener<S: SegmentSink, I: IsnSource>(
            &var self, id: ConnId, addr: Ipv4Addr, remote: SocketAddr,
            now: UInt64, isn: &var I, sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, OpenError> {
        let i = try self.used(id) catch { return OpenError.NotListening }
        if self.slots[i].state != State.Listen {
            return OpenError.NotListening
        }
        let local = SocketAddr(addr: addr, port: self.slots[i].local.port)
        try self.check_open(local, remote)
        self.abort_spawned(i, &var sink)
        let iss = isn.isn(local, remote)
        self.slots[i] = Connection.begin(State.SynSent, local, remote, iss,
                                         self.min_rto)
        let _ = self.slots[i].output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.2's SEND on connection `id` at `now`: copies as much
    /// of `data` as its send buffer has room for, sends what may go, and
    /// answers how much it took.
    public func write<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, id: ConnId, data: &[UInt8],
                                      now: UInt64,
                                      sink: &var S) -> Result<Int, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        self.slots[i].write(&data, now, &var sink)
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.3's RECEIVE on connection `id` at `now`: copies what
    /// was received in order into `into`, as much as fits, and answers how
    /// much; 0 once the peer has closed and nothing is left, and `WouldBlock`
    /// while nothing waits.
    public func read<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, id: ConnId,
                                     into: &var [UInt8], now: UInt64,
                                     sink: &var S) -> Result<Int, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        self.slots[i].read(&var into, now, &var sink)
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.4's CLOSE of connection or listener `id` at `now`. A
    /// listener's connections not yet accepted are aborted with it.
    public func close<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, id: ConnId, now: UInt64,
                                      sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        if self.slots[i].state == State.Listen {
            self.abort_spawned(i, &var sink)
        }
        self.slots[i].close(now, &var sink)
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.5's ABORT of connection or listener `id`: it is
    /// CLOSED at once, and the peer, if it knows of the connection, is sent
    /// a reset. A listener's connections not yet accepted are aborted with
    /// it.
    public func abort<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, id: ConnId,
                                      sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        if self.slots[i].state == State.Listen {
            self.abort_spawned(i, &var sink)
        }
        self.slots[i].abort(&var sink)
    }

    /// Takes `segment`, the whole IP payload of a datagram from `src` to
    /// `dst` received at `now`, to the connection or listener it is for, and
    /// sends whatever it calls for. Answers why it was not taken as it came,
    /// when it was not: why it was dropped, or that it was a reset that ended
    /// its connection.
    public func receive<S: SegmentSink, I: IsnSource>(
            &var self, src: Ipv4Addr, dst: Ipv4Addr, segment: &[UInt8],
            now: UInt64, isn: &var I, sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, TcpDrop> {
        let parsed = try tcp.Received(segment: &segment, src: src, dst: dst)
        let header = parsed.header
        let data = parsed.data_bytes()
        if header.src_port == 0 || header.dst_port == 0 {
            return TcpDrop.BadPort
        }
        let local = SocketAddr(addr: dst, port: header.dst_port)
        let remote = SocketAddr(addr: src, port: header.src_port)
        if let i = self.find_slot(local, remote) {
            return self.slots[i].arrive(&header, &segment[parsed.data_at()..],
                                        now, &var sink)
        }
        if let l = self.listener(header.dst_port) {
            return self.arrive_listen(l, local, remote, &header, data, now,
                                      &var isn, &var sink)
        }
        // RFC 9293 §3.10.7.1: no connection.
        if header.has(RST) {
            return TcpDrop.NoConnection
        }
        reset_reply(local, remote, &header, data, &var sink)
        return TcpDrop.NoConnection
    }

    /// Sends at `now` whatever each connection owes and may send. Answers
    /// `Full` when the sink refused a segment: what is left is owed still, and
    /// the next call sends it.
    public func output<S: SegmentSink>(
            &var self, now: UInt64,
            sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, SegmentError> {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            try self.slots[i].output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
            i = i + 1
        }
        return
    }

    /// Ages every connection at `now`: ends each TIME-WAIT that has lasted
    /// `TIME_WAIT_MS`, retransmits for each timer that expired or gives the
    /// connection up, and sends data the silly window rule has held back for
    /// `SWS_OVERRIDE_MS`.
    public func poll<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, now: UInt64, sink: &var S) {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            self.slots[i].tick(now, &var sink)
            i = i + 1
        }
    }

    /// The state of connection or listener `id`: CLOSED once it has ended.
    public func state(&self, id: ConnId) -> State {
        let i = try self.used(id) catch { return State.Closed }
        self.slots[i].state
    }

    /// Connection or listener `id`'s state, sequence numbers and timers.
    public func status(&self, id: ConnId) -> Result<Status, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        return self.slots[i].status()
    }

    /// The connection between `local` and `remote`, or `None`.
    public func find(&self, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr) -> ConnId? {
        if let i = self.find_slot(local, remote) {
            return ConnId(slot: i as UInt8, generation: self.generations[i])
        }
        None
    }

    /// A port of the dynamic range no connection or listener uses, searched
    /// from where the last search stopped. One is found within
    /// `CONNECTIONS + 1` tries: at most `CONNECTIONS` are used.
    public func ephemeral_port(&var self) -> UInt16 {
        var candidate = self.next_port
        while self.uses_port(candidate) {
            candidate = after(candidate)
        }
        self.next_port = after(candidate)
        candidate
    }

    /// Moves connection `id`'s send sequence on by `by`, as if that much had
    /// been sent and acknowledged: for a test that reaches the far end of the
    /// sequence space without sending gigabytes. Only an established
    /// connection with nothing queued or in flight moves; any other answers
    /// `WouldBlock`.
    public func skip_sequence(&var self, id: ConnId,
                              by: UInt32) -> Result<Void, CallError> {
        let i = try self.used(id)
        self.slots[i].skip_sequence(by)
    }

    /// Whether a connection or listener uses `port` as its own.
    public func uses_port(&self, port: UInt16) -> Bool {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.slots[i].state != State.Closed &&
               self.slots[i].local.port == port {
                return true
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        false
    }

    /// RFC 9293 §3.10.7.2: a segment to listener `l`. A SYN spawns a
    /// connection in SYN-RECEIVED that answers with SYN,ACK. Text and FIN on
    /// the SYN are not kept; the peer sends them again.
    func arrive_listen<S: SegmentSink, I: IsnSource>(
            &var self, l: Int, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr,
            header: &Header, data: Int, now: UInt64, isn: &var I,
            sink: &var S) -> Result<Void, TcpDrop> {
        if header.has(RST) {
            return TcpDrop.ResetIgnored
        }
        if header.has(ACK) {
            reset_reply(local, remote, &header, data, &var sink)
            return TcpDrop.AckUnsent
        }
        if not header.has(SYN) {
            return TcpDrop.NoSyn
        }
        guard let i = self.free_slot() else { return TcpDrop.TableFull }
        let iss = isn.isn(local, remote)
        var c = Connection.begin(State.SynReceived, local, remote, iss,
                                 self.min_rto)
        c.passive = true
        c.listener = l
        c.take_syn(&header)
        let _ = self.occupy(i, c)
        let _ = self.slots[i].output(now, &var sink, Push.Normal)
        return
    }

    /// Whether slot `i` holds a connection the listener in slot `l` spawned.
    func spawned_by(&self, i: Int, l: Int) -> Bool {
        self.slots[i].state != State.Closed && self.slots[i].passive &&
            self.slots[i].listener == l
    }

    /// Aborts every connection the listener in slot `l` spawned that was not
    /// handed out.
    func abort_spawned<S: SegmentSink>(&var self, l: Int, sink: &var S) {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.spawned_by(i, l) && not self.slots[i].accepted {
                let _ = self.slots[i].abort(&var sink)
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }

    /// Refuses an open between `local` and `remote` that cannot be one.
    func check_open(&self, local: SocketAddr,
                    remote: SocketAddr) -> Result<Void, OpenError> {
        if local.port == 0 || remote.port == 0 ||
           local.addr.is_unspecified() || remote.addr.is_unspecified() {
            return OpenError.BadAddress
        }
        if self.find_slot(local, remote) != None {
            return OpenError.InUse
        }
        return
    }

    /// Puts `c` in free slot `i` as its next occupant, and answers its id.
    func occupy(&var self, i: Int, c: Connection) -> ConnId {
        self.generations[i] = self.generations[i] &+ 1
        self.slots[i] = c
        ConnId(slot: i as UInt8, generation: self.generations[i])
    }

    /// The slot of `id` while it holds the connection or listener `id` names;
    /// once that has ended, why.
    func used(&self, id: ConnId) -> Result<Int, CallError> {
        let i = id.slot as Int
        if self.generations[i] != id.generation {
            return CallError.NoConnection
        }
        if self.slots[i].state == State.Closed {
            return self.slots[i].ended
        }
        return i
    }

    /// The slot of the connection between `local` and `remote`. A listener
    /// is none: its addresses are 0.0.0.0, which no connection's are.
    func find_slot(&self, local: SocketAddr, remote: SocketAddr) -> Int? {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.slots[i].state != State.Closed &&
               self.slots[i].local == local && self.slots[i].remote == remote {
                return i
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        None
    }

    /// The slot of the listener on `port`.
    func listener(&self, port: UInt16) -> Int? {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.slots[i].state == State.Listen &&
               self.slots[i].local.port == port {
                return i
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        None
    }

    func free_slot(&self) -> Int? {
        var i = 0
        while i < CONNECTIONS {
            if self.slots[i].state == State.Closed &&
               not self.slots[i].reset_owed {
                return i
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        None
    }
}
